4. Time Quantum

Time Quantum is a concept in TLM2 (Transaction Level Modeling 2) that relates to temporal decoupling and the management of simulation time in loosely-timed models. Here are the key points:

 Definition*:
A Time Quantum is the maximum amount of time that a process (typically an initiator) is allowed to run ahead of the global simulation time.

 Purpose*:
It helps balance simulation efficiency with timing accuracy in loosely-timed models.
Allows processes to execute multiple transactions without constantly synchronizing with the global simulation time.

 Temporal Decoupling*:
Time Quantum is a key component of temporal decoupling, where processes can run ahead of the global simulation time.
This improves simulation performance by reducing the number of context switches between processes.

 Synchronization*:
When a process reaches its time quantum limit, it must synchronize with the global simulation time.
This ensures that all processes stay relatively close in simulated time, maintaining overall timing consistency.

 Flexibility*:
The size of the time quantum can be adjusted based on the needs of the simulation.
Smaller quanta provide more accurate timing but may slow down simulation; larger quanta improve performance but may reduce timing accuracy.

