5. Temporal Decoupling

Temporal Decoupling is a key feature in TLM2 (Transaction Level Modeling 2) that allows different processes or components in a simulation to run ahead of the global simulation time within a predefined time quantum. This feature is closely related to the Time Quantum concept we discussed earlier.

 Key aspects of Temporal Decoupling*:
   Local Time*:

   Time Quantum*:

   Synchronization*:

   Performance Improvement*:

   Loose Timing*:

* Each process maintains its own local time, which can advance independently of the global simulation time.
* A predefined time period within which a process can run ahead without synchronizing with the global time.
* Processes synchronize their local time with the global time when they reach the end of their time quantum or when explicitly required.
* By reducing the number of context switches and synchronizations, temporal decoupling can significantly speed up simulations.
* It's particularly useful in loosely-timed models where precise cycle-accurate timing is not required.
