6. Transport Interfaces

Transport Interfaces are a key feature of TLM2 that define how transactions are communicated between components. TLM2 provides two main types of transport interfaces:

 Blocking Transport Interface*:
Implemented via the b_transport method.
Simpler to use and understand.
The entire transaction is completed in a single function call.
The initiator waits for the target to complete the transaction.

 Non-Blocking Transport Interface*:
Implemented via the nb_transport_fw and nb_transport_bw methods.
More complex but offers greater flexibility.
Allows for multiple phases in a transaction.
The initiator can continue processing while waiting for the target's response.

These interfaces allow for different levels of timing accuracy and simulation performance.

